PATCH 203: base-loss fix for the SECOND operand in FUN_1000_8daa -- the d550 detail-LOD lookup
(asm 0x18dd3..0x18ded), exposed once patch 201/202 let 8daa run past the a80b context load.

8daa's first-entry branch computes DAT_1000_d550 from the current object + the STRSEG LOD table:
    mov ax,[di+0x89]; add ax,0x400; mov bl,ah; and bx,0xf8; shr bx,1; shr bx,1;
    mov gs,[0x70]; mov ax,gs:[bx+0x2700]; mov [0x1550],ax
di = iVar1 = DAT_2000_2d34 (object near-offset, 0 on the setup pass), so `[di+0x89]` is DS=DGROUP
relative and `gs:[bx+0x2700]` is STRSEG (gs=word[DGROUP:0x70]) relative.  Ghidra dropped BOTH bases:
`*(int *)(iVar1 + 0x89)` deref'd absolute host 0x89 (SIGSEGV) and the table read `*(undefined2*)(..+
0x2700)` lost the STRSEG segment.  Rebase the object read onto DGROUP and the table read onto STRSEG.

Reached on the ACCEPT cascade immediately after patch 202 (a80b returns, 8daa continues to d550).
Behaviour-neutral for the 19 verify flows (mission viewport never entered).  Observed: the 0x89
SIGSEGV in 8daa is gone; d550/d552 are set and 8daa RETURNS -> the 4308 table walk continues.
Sources stay pristine; fist.c via build/.

--- a/fist.c
+++ b/fist.c
@@ -59848,6 +59848,15 @@
       FUN_1000_a80b(0xf,iVar1);
       DAT_1000_d54a = 4;
-      DAT_1000_d550 = *(undefined2 *)(((*(int *)(iVar1 + 0x89) + 0x400U >> 8 & 0xf8) >> 2) + 0x2700)
-      ;
+      /* PATCH 203: d550 detail-LOD lookup (asm 0x18dd3): ax=word[DGROUP:di+0x89]+0x400;
+         idx=((ax>>8)&0xf8)>>2; d550=word[STRSEG:idx+0x2700] (STRSEG=word[DGROUP:0x70]).  Ghidra
+         base-lost the object read (absolute 0x89 SIGSEGV) and the STRSEG table read. */
+      {
+        uint8_t *dg = g_mem + 0x1c000;
+        uint16_t di = (uint16_t)iVar1;
+        uint16_t ax = (uint16_t)(*(uint16_t *)(dg + (uint16_t)(di + 0x89)) + 0x400);
+        uint16_t idx = (uint16_t)(((ax >> 8) & 0xf8) >> 2);
+        uint16_t strseg = *(uint16_t *)(dg + 0x70);
+        DAT_1000_d550 = *(uint16_t *)(g_mem + ((uint32_t)strseg << 4) + (uint16_t)(idx + 0x2700));
+      }
       DAT_1000_d552 = 0x9134;
       return;
